Q1. What is CSS Box Model?
The CSS Box Model describes how every HTML element is represented as a rectangular box. It consists of four parts: content, padding, border, and margin.

• Content: The actual text, image, or other content of the element.
• Padding: Space between the content and the border.
• Border: The line surrounding the content and padding.
• Margin: Space outside the border, separating the element from other elements.

Example:
.box {
  width: 200px;
  padding: 20px;
  border: 2px solid black;
  margin: 10px;
}

By default, the given width applies to the content area, while padding and border are added to the total size.
Q2. What is media query? Explain with a sample code.
A media query is a CSS feature used to apply different styles depending on the screen size or other device conditions. It is mainly used to create responsive websites.

Example:
body {
  background: white;
}

@media (max-width: 600px) {
  body {
    background: lightblue;
  }
}

In this example, the background changes to light blue when the screen width is 600px or less. This helps a website adjust its design for mobile, tablet, and desktop screens.
Q3. What are the features of Bootstrap?
Bootstrap is a popular frontend framework used to create responsive and mobile-friendly websites.

Main features of Bootstrap are:
1. Responsive grid system.
2. Ready-made components such as buttons, cards, forms and navbars.
3. Utility classes for spacing, colors, alignment and display.
4. Mobile-first design.
5. Responsive breakpoints for different screen sizes.
6. JavaScript components such as modals, dropdowns and carousels.
7. Easy customization using CSS and Sass.
8. Reduces development time by providing commonly used styles and components.
Q4. Write an example of basic grid structure in Bootstrap?
Bootstrap uses a 12-column grid system. The basic structure normally contains a container, a row, and columns.

Example:
<div class="container">
  <div class="row">
    <div class="col-md-4">Column 1</div>
    <div class="col-md-4">Column 2</div>
    <div class="col-md-4">Column 3</div>
  </div>
</div>

Here, each col-md-4 takes 4 out of the 12 available columns, so the three columns together occupy the complete row.
Q5. What is CSS Selectors?
CSS selectors are used to select HTML elements on which CSS styles are applied.

Common types of selectors are:
1. Element selector: p { color: blue; }
2. Class selector: .box { padding: 10px; }
3. ID selector: #header { background: black; }
4. Universal selector: * { margin: 0; }
5. Group selector: h1, h2, p { font-family: Arial; }
6. Attribute selector: input[type="text"] { border: 1px solid gray; }

Selectors make it possible to target specific elements or groups of elements on a webpage.
Q6. What are CSS backgrounds, list the properties?
CSS background properties are used to control the background of an HTML element.

Important background properties are:
• background-color – sets the background color.
• background-image – adds a background image.
• background-repeat – controls whether the image repeats.
• background-position – sets the position of the image.
• background-size – controls the size of the background image.
• background-attachment – controls whether the background scrolls with the page.
• background-origin – specifies the area used for positioning the background.
• background-clip – controls how far the background extends.
• background – shorthand property for setting several background values.

Example:
.box {
  background-color: lightgray;
  background-image: url("image.jpg");
  background-size: cover;
  background-position: center;
}
Q7. How can we overlap elements in CSS?
Elements can be overlapped in CSS by using positioning properties and the z-index property.

Example:
.box1 {
  position: absolute;
  left: 50px;
  top: 50px;
  z-index: 1;
}

.box2 {
  position: absolute;
  left: 80px;
  top: 80px;
  z-index: 2;
}

Since box2 has a higher z-index value, it appears above box1. Overlapping can also be achieved using negative margins, transforms, or CSS Grid.
Q8. What are the different positioning properties in CSS?
The main values of the CSS position property are:

1. static – The default position. The element follows the normal document flow.
2. relative – The element remains in the normal flow but can be moved using top, right, bottom, or left.
3. absolute – The element is removed from the normal flow and is positioned relative to its nearest positioned ancestor.
4. fixed – The element is positioned relative to the browser window and remains in the same place while scrolling.
5. sticky – The element behaves like a relatively positioned element until a given scroll position is reached, after which it sticks in place.

Example:
.box {
  position: relative;
  top: 20px;
  left: 10px;
}
Q9. What is ECMAScript?
ECMAScript is a standard specification that defines the rules and features of the JavaScript language. JavaScript is the most widely used implementation of ECMAScript.

ECMAScript is maintained as a language standard and is updated through different editions. Features such as let, const, arrow functions, classes, promises, and modules are included in modern ECMAScript versions.

In simple words, ECMAScript is the standard, while JavaScript is an implementation of that standard used in browsers and other environments.
Q10. What is the difference between var, let and const in JavaScript. Explain Temporal Dead zone?
var, let, and const are used to declare variables in JavaScript, but they have different rules.

• var is function-scoped. It can be redeclared and reassigned.
• let is block-scoped. It can be reassigned but cannot be redeclared in the same block.
• const is block-scoped. It cannot be reassigned after initialization and must be initialized when declared.

Example:
var a = 10;
var a = 20;       // allowed

let b = 10;
b = 20;           // allowed

const c = 10;
// c = 20;        // error

Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ):
The Temporal Dead Zone is the period from entering a block until the declaration of a let or const variable is reached. During this period, the variable cannot be accessed.

Example:
console.log(x);   // ReferenceError
let x = 10;

The error occurs because x is in the Temporal Dead Zone before its declaration is reached.
